## RULES

Follow these rules exactly. Do not skip any rule.

**Rule 1 — Decompose the question into concepts.** Identify the concepts (skills, projects, performance, experience, salary, education, leave, identity, etc.) and the filters (specific entities, IDs, departments, dates).

**Rule 2 — Expand each concept to ALL the column types that can carry it.**
A concept is almost never in one column. For each concept, include the FULL list of matching column types below. **You must select ALL column types that exist in the schema for each concept — do not stop after one match.**

**CONCEPT → COLUMN TYPE checklist (select every present type):**
- **skills / competencies / abilities** → competency/skill name structs, competency RATING structs, work-experience structs (responsibilities/duties = applied skills), achievement structs, professional-summary/profile fields, education/certification/qualification structs.
- **projects / assignments** → assignment-history structs, objectives/goals structs, work-experience structs (CV projects/responsibilities), achievements/recognition structs, competency-rating structs (assessed in project context).
- **performance / appraisal** → performance-rating structs, objectives/goals structs, achievement/comment structs, competency-rating structs.
- **experience / tenure / history** → work-experience structs, assignment-history structs, previous-employer structs, join-date/tenure columns.
- **education / qualification** → education structs, qualification/certification structs, institute/degree columns.
- **salary / compensation** → the total/aggregate salary column (include the total, not its component allowances, unless specific components are named).

> **KEY RULE for multi-struct concepts (skills, projects, performance, experience):** these span MULTIPLE struct groups. After you find the first matching struct, KEEP scanning the schema for more. For example, a "skills" question needs competency names AND ratings AND work experience AND achievements AND a professional summary AND education. **Do not stop after one.** Check that you did not miss a summary/profile, experience, or achievement group.

### Rule 3 — Scan ALL columns and select every relevant one.
Go through the schema one column at a time. Include a column if its name, description, type, or inner fields relate to any concept or checklist above.
- When a struct is relevant, include the **entire group with all inner fields**.
- **Check every struct group** — even if one already matches, continue through the rest.

### Rule 4 — Apply include/exclude.
**Include:**
- Columns for **filtering** (identifiers the question mentions).
- Columns for **display** or **calculation**.
- For a named entity: **both the primary identifier and a human-readable name**.
- When a struct is relevant: **all inner fields**; include **multiple** structs if each is relevant.
- For comprehensive questions ("all", "compare", "everything"): include **more**.

**Exclude:**
- Columns unrelated to any concept.
- Personal/contextual attributes (demographics, contact info) unless explicitly asked.
- **Redundant breakdowns**: if a total/aggregate exists, skip its individual components unless named.
- Near-duplicates: prefer the best match; include both only if ambiguous.

**Default: when in doubt, include.** Over-selection is safer than under-selection.

